modules via `_sanity_check` and returns `sys.modules[name]` for dotted names... actually `__import__` with fromlist handles it).\n\nLet me think: `__import__('ansible.utils.display', globals, locals, ['Display'], 0)`. The C implementation `import_name` \u2192 for `from ... import` with fromlist, it calls `import_module_level`... it does `_PyImport_GetModuleAttrString`... Actually in CPython, `PyImport_ImportModuleLevel` \u2192 if fromlist is set, it imports the module and returns the module itself; the submodule lookup: it calls `import_module_level` which calls `PyImport_Import`-like logic: it checks `sys.modules` for the full name via `import_get_module`. If found and it's a module, it returns it (after checking `_handle_fromlist` for the fromlist names \u2014 but since we return the module and then `from X import Y` does getattr on the returned object... wait no).\n\nActually the bytecode for `from ansible.utils.display import Display` is:\n```\nIMPORT_NAME ansible.utils.display (fromlist=('Display',))\nIMPORT_FROM Display\n```\n`IMPORT_NAME` calls `__import__('ansible.utils.display', ..., fromlist=('Display',))`. In CPython's `import_name` \u2192 `PyImport_ImportModuleLevelObject`: it first tries `import_get_module(tstate, abs_name)` \u2014 checks sys.modules. If found, and fromlist is set, it calls `_handle_fromlist(module, fromlist, ...)`. `_handle_fromlist` checks if `Display` is an attribute of the module; if not, it tries to import submodule `ansible.utils.display.Display` \u2014 which would fail... but our fake module has `Display` as an attribute, so `_handle_fromlist` finds it and returns the module. Then `IMPORT_FROM` does getattr(module, 'Display'). \n\nBut there's a wrinkle: `import_get_module` checks `sys.modules[abs_name]` \u2014 but also, before that, `PyImport_ImportModuleLevelObject` may need parent package? For absolute imports of dotted names, if 'ansible.utils.display' is in sys.modules, it's returned directly without importing parents. I believe that's correct \u2014 sys.modules hit short-circuits.\n\nHowever, there's a risk: if `ansible.utils.display` was ALREADY imported (e.g., by other tests or conftest), monkeypatch.setitem replaces it, and after the test it's restored. During the test, `from ansible.utils.display import Display` gets the fake. Good.\n\nBut also `ansible` and `ansible.utils` packages: `unsafe_proxy.py` is in `ansible.utils` package, so `ansible` and `ansible.utils` are already imported. The from-import only needs 'ansible.utils.display' in sys.modules. Good.\n\nWhich approach is safer? Patching the real module's attribute requires the real module to import successfully. Patching sys.modules avoids importing the real module entirely. The sys.modules approach is more robust. But there's a subtle issue: if `ansible.utils.display` is already in sys.modules and some other code holds a direct reference to the real Display class... doesn't matter for our test.\n\nActually, hmm, one more subtlety with the sys.modules approach: `monkeypatch.setitem(sys.modules, 'ansible.utils.display', fake_module)` \u2014 if the key doesn't exist, monkeypatch records that and removes it after. Good cleanup either way.\n\nI'll go with the sys.modules approach as primary since it's fully isolated. Actually, let me combine: use monkeypatch.setitem on sys.modules. That's clean.\n\nWait, but there's one more consideration \u2014 `_handle_fromlist`: when the module in sys.modules is found and fromlist=('Display',), `_handle_fromlist` checks `hasattr(module, 'Display')` \u2014 wait, actually it checks `if not hasattr(module, x)` then tries `import submodule`. Our fake has the attribute, so fine.\n\nHmm, actually let me double-check CPython behavior for `from X.Y import Z` when `sys.modules['X.Y']` exists but `X` and `X.Y`... `import_get_module` returns sys.modules['X.Y'] if present. Then since fromlist is not NULL, `_handle_fromlist` is called. Then returns the module. Then IMPORT_FROM gets 'Display'. Yes, this works. This is a well-known mocking technique (e.g., `mock.patch('sys.modules', ...)` or `mock.patch.dict(sys.modules, ...)`).\n\nActually, even simpler and very common: `monkeypatch.setattr('ansible.utils.display.Display', fake_class)`. This requires importing the real module. The real `ansible.utils.display` module imports fine in normal ansible environments (it's used by CLI). I think either works. Let me use the sys.modules approach to be maximally isolated and avoid any side effects of importing display (e.g., it might set up locale or check CRYPTOGRAPHY... Display imports `from ansible.module_utils.common.text.converters import to_text` etc. \u2014 harmless).\n\nHmm, actually there's one potential issue with the sys.modules approach: `ansible.utils.display` might already be imported by something else, and the real module object stays referenced elsewhere \u2014 but our patch only affects the import inside `__new__`. Fine.\n\nLet me also double check: does `unsafe_proxy.py` itself import Display at module level? From the get_info output, no \u2014 only `to_text` and `string_types`. Good.\n\nNow, about `to_text` with `errors='surrogate_or_strict'`: for bytes b'foo', returns u'foo'. For a str input u'foo', to_text returns it as-is (text). Good.\n\nLet me also think about whether `UnsafeProxy(u'foo')` triggers `__init__` issues \u2014 as analyzed, no, because result is not an instance of UnsafeProxy. But wait, what about the non-string passthrough case where result is a list \u2014 not an UnsafeProxy instance, so no `__init__` call. And for `AnsibleUnsafeText` result \u2014 not an UnsafeProxy instance. Good.\n\nHmm wait, actually there's one more subtle thing: when `__new__` returns an object that IS an instance of cls, `__init__` gets called with the original args. None of our cases return an UnsafeProxy instance. Good.\n\nAlso, should I add a test for `UnsafeProxy` being called with an `AnsibleUnsafe` subclass instance created via `wrap_var`? Not necessary.\n\nLet me also make sure the deprecation assertion is robust: the fake records one call per UnsafeProxy instantiation. In the non-string test with 4 objects, 4 calls. Good.\n\nOne more: to be extra safe about `Display()` being constructed with no arguments \u2014 my lambda `lambda *a, **kw: fake` handles it.\n\nAlso, I want to verify that the tests indeed execute lines 88-102. Test 1: lines 88-91 (import, Display(), deprecated), 97 (isinstance check, False), 100 (isinstance string, True), 101 (wrap), 102 (return). Test 3/4: line 97 True, 98 return. Test 5: 97 False, 100 False, 102 return. All lines covered. Branches: 97 both ways, 100 both ways. \n\nNow write the final script. Keep top-level minimal: imports, fake class, fixture, tests. No pytest.main.\n\nAlso, should I import `binary_type` from six for an assertion? Not strictly needed. I'll assert `isinstance(result, AnsibleUnsafe)` and `result is obj`.\n\nLet me also consider: `AnsibleUnsafeBytes(b'foo')` \u2014 constructing it directly is fine since it's just binary_type subclass.\n\nFinal code:\n\n```python\nimport sys\nimport types\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom ansible.utils.unsafe_proxy import (\n    AnsibleUnsafe,\n    AnsibleUnsafeBytes,\n    AnsibleUnsafeText,\n    UnsafeProxy,\n)\n\nDEPRECATION_MSG = u'UnsafeProxy is being deprecated. Use wrap_var or AnsibleUnsafeBytes/AnsibleUnsafeText directly instead'\n\n\nclass _FakeDisplay(object):\n    \"\"\"Records deprecated() calls instead of writing to the display.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, *args, **kwargs):\n        self.deprecated_calls = []\n\n    def deprecated(self, msg, version=None, removed=False, collection_name=None, **kwargs):\n        self.deprecated_calls.append({\n            'msg': msg,\n            'version': version,\n            'removed': removed,\n            'collection_name': collection_name,\n        })\n\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef fake_display(monkeypatch):\n    \"\"\"Replace ansible.utils.display with a stub module so UnsafeProxy.__new__'s\n    lazy ``from ansible.utils.display import Display`` picks up the fake.\"\"\"\n    fake = _FakeDisplay()\n    stub_module = types.ModuleType(u'ansible.utils.display')\n    stub_module.Display = lambda *args, **kwargs: fake\n    monkeypatch.setitem(sys.modules, u'ansible.utils.display', stub_module)\n    return fake\n\n\ndef test_unsafe_proxy_wraps_text_and_deprecates(fake_display):\n    \"\"\"Lines 88-91, 100-102: a text string is wrapped in AnsibleUnsafeText.\"\"\"\n    result = UnsafeProxy(u'foo')\n\n    assert type(result) is AnsibleUnsafeText\n    assert result == u'foo'\n    assert result.__UNSAFE__ is True\n    assert fake_display.deprecated_calls == [{\n        'msg': DEPRECATION_MSG,\n        'version': u'2.13',\n        'removed': False,\n        'collection_name': u'ansible.builtin',\n    }]\n\n\ndef test_unsafe_proxy_converts_bytes_to_text(fake_display):\n    \"\"\"Lines 100-101: bytes input is converted with to_text before wrapping.\"\"\"\n    result = UnsafeProxy(b'foo')\n\n    assert type(result) is AnsibleUnsafeText\n    assert result == u'foo'\n    assert result.__UNSAFE__ is True\n    assert len(fake_display.deprecated_calls) == 1\n\n\ndef test_unsafe_proxy_returns_existing_unsafe_text_unchanged(fake_display):\n    \"\"\"Lines 97-98: an already-unsafe object short-circuits and is returned as-is.\"\"\"\n    obj = AnsibleUnsafeText(u'foo')\n    result = UnsafeProxy(obj)\n\n    assert result is obj\n    assert isinstance(result, AnsibleUnsafe)\n    assert len(fake_display.deprecated_calls) == 1\n\n\ndef test_unsafe_proxy_returns_existing_unsafe_bytes_unchanged(fake_display):\n    \"\"\"Lines 97-98: unsafe bytes are not re-wrapped into AnsibleUnsafeText.\"\"\"\n    obj = AnsibleUnsafeBytes(b'foo')\n    result = UnsafeProxy(obj)\n\n    assert result is obj\n    assert isinstance(result, AnsibleUnsafe)\n    assert len(fake_display.deprecated_calls) == 1\n\n\ndef test_unsafe_proxy_passes_through_non_string_objects(fake_display):\n    \"\"\"Line 102: non-string, non-unsafe objects are returned unchanged.\"\"\"\n    passthrough_objects = [[u'foo'], {u'key': u'value'}, 42, None]\n\n    for obj in passthrough_objects:\n        result = UnsafeProxy(obj)\n        assert result is obj\n        assert not isinstance(result, AnsibleUnsafe)\n\n    assert len(fake_display.deprecated_calls) == len(passthrough_objects)\n```\n\nWait \u2014 one issue with `test_unsafe_proxy_passes_through_non_string_objects` and `None`: `UnsafeProxy(None)` \u2014 `isinstance(None, AnsibleUnsafe)` False, `isinstance(None, string_types)` False \u2192 return None. Good.\n\nHmm, but wait: `UnsafeProxy(42)` \u2014 `__new__` returns 42, not an instance of UnsafeProxy, so no `__init__`. Good.\n\nOne concern about the `removed` kwarg: in some ansible versions, `Display.deprecated` signature is `deprecated(self, msg, version=None, removed=False, collection_name='ansible.builtin')`. In even newer ones (post 2.13?), it might be different, but since our fake defines its own signature and the call site only passes msg, version, collection_name as keywords, our fake handles it regardless of the real Display's signature. Good.\n\nAnother consideration: should the fixture also handle the case where `ansible.utils.display` real module import is needed by other machinery? No.\n\nAlso, is there a risk that `UnsafeProxy.__new__`'s `from ansible.utils.display import Display` fails because `ansible.utils` package's `__init__` does something? `ansible.utils` is already imported (we import unsafe_proxy from it). The from-import with sys.modules hit doesn't re-execute package code. Good.\n\nActually, hold on \u2014 let me reconsider `monkeypatch.setitem(sys.modules, ...)`. If `ansible.utils.display` is NOT in sys.modules initially, monkeypatch.setitem will add it and remove it after. If it IS present, it will restore the original. Both fine.\n\nBut one more subtlety: after `monkeypatch.setitem(sys.modules, 'ansible.utils.display', stub)`, if some other import during the test 