        "reasoning": "Now I understand the code. Let me write tests:\n\n1. Test with a string input: covers lines 88-91 (Display deprecation), 100-101 (string conversion), 102 (return). Assert the result is an `AnsibleUnsafeText` with the correct value and `__UNSAFE__` is True. Also assert the deprecation was called with the right args.\n\n2. Test with an `AnsibleUnsafe` instance (e.g., `AnsibleUnsafeText` or `AnsibleUnsafeBytes`): covers lines 97-98 (early return). Assert the same object is returned.\n\n3. Test with a non-string, non-unsafe object (e.g., a list or int): covers line 102 (return obj unchanged). Assert the object is returned as-is.\n\nI need to mock `Display` to avoid actual deprecation output and to verify the call. The import is inside `__new__`: `from ansible.utils.display import Display`. So I need to patch `ansible.utils.display.Display` with monkeypatch.\n\nLet me write the test:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\n\nfrom ansible.module_utils.six import text_type, binary_type\nfrom ansible.utils.unsafe_proxy import (\n    AnsibleUnsafe,\n    AnsibleUnsafeBytes,\n    AnsibleUnsafeText,\n    UnsafeProxy,\n)\n\n\nclass _FakeDisplay(object):\n    \"\"\"Stand-in for ansible.utils.display.Display that records deprecation calls.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, *args, **kwargs):\n        self.calls = []\n\n    def deprecated(self, msg, version=None, collection_name=None, **kwargs):\n        self.calls.append((msg, version, collection_name))\n\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef fake_display(monkeypatch):\n    \"\"\"Patch Display inside ansible.utils.display so UnsafeProxy.__new__ uses it.\"\"\"\n    instance = _FakeDisplay()\n    monkeypatch.setattr('ansible.utils.display.Display', lambda *a, **kw: instance)\n    return instance\n\n\ndef test_unsafe_proxy_string(fake_display):\n    \"\"\"UnsafeProxy on a str wraps it in AnsibleUnsafeText and emits the deprecation.\"\"\"\n    result = UnsafeProxy(u'foo')\n    assert isinstance(result, AnsibleUnsafeText)\n    assert isinstance(result, text_type)\n    assert result == u'foo'\n    assert result.__UNSAFE__ is True\n    # deprecation was emitted exactly once with the expected parameters\n    assert fake_display.calls == [\n        (u'UnsafeProxy is being deprecated. Use wrap_var or AnsibleUnsafeBytes/AnsibleUnsafeText directly instead',\n         u'2.13', u'ansible.builtin'),\n    ]\n\n\ndef test_unsafe_proxy_bytes(fake_display):\n    \"\"\"UnsafeProxy on bytes converts to text via to_text and wraps in AnsibleUnsafeText.\"\"\"\n    result = UnsafeProxy(b'foo')\n    assert isinstance(result, AnsibleUnsafeText)\n    assert result == u'foo'\n    assert result.__UNSAFE__ is True\n    assert len(fake_display.calls) == 1\n\n\ndef test_unsafe_proxy_passthrough_unsafe(fake_display):\n    \"\"\"An already-unsafe object is returned as-is (lines 97-98).\"\"\"\n    obj = AnsibleUnsafeText(u'foo')\n    result = UnsafeProxy(obj)\n    assert result is obj\n    assert isinstance(result, AnsibleUnsafe)\n    assert len(fake_display.calls) == 1\n\n\ndef test_unsafe_proxy_passthrough_unsafe_bytes(fake_display):\n    obj = AnsibleUnsafeBytes(b'foo')\n    result = UnsafeProxy(obj)\n    assert result is obj\n    assert isinstance(result, AnsibleUnsafe)\n\n\ndef test_unsafe_proxy_non_string(fake_display):\n    \"\"\"Non-string, non-unsafe objects pass through unchanged (line 102).\"\"\"\n    obj = [u'foo']\n    result = UnsafeProxy(obj)\n    assert result is obj\n    assert not isinstance(result, AnsibleUnsafe)\n    assert len(fake_display.calls) == 1\n```\n\nWait \u2014 one issue: `AnsibleUnsafeText` is a subclass of `text_type` (unicode on py2, str on py3). `isinstance(result, text_type)` \u2014 on py3, `text_type` is `str`. That's fine.\n\nAlso, `to_text(b'foo', errors='surrogate_or_strict')` gives u'foo'. Good.\n\nOne concern: `monkeypatch.setattr('ansible.utils.display.Display', ...)` \u2014 the import inside `__new__` is `from ansible.utils.display import Display`, which happens at call time, so patching t